<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  tingra-cli.entitlements
  tingra-cli

  Created by Larry Aasen on 2026-07-09.
  Copyright © 2026 Larry Aasen.
  SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

  Applied by scripts/release-cli-package.sh at signing (see CLI.md, "Distribution").
  Notarization requires the hardened runtime, which denies camera and
  microphone access unless the binary opts in — hence the two device
  entitlements. disable-library-validation lets the host load third-party
  plug-ins signed by other teams (ARCHITECTURE.md). No sandbox entitlement:
  Tingra is deliberately unsandboxed.

  Every entitlement here is unrestricted — one the hardened runtime grants on
  the signature alone. That constraint is the whole shape of this file, and it
  is not a style preference: the kernel authorizes a *restricted* entitlement
  from an embedded provisioning profile, and a bare Mach-O executable has
  nowhere to carry one (embedded.provisionprofile lives inside a bundle's
  Contents/). codesign does not check, so a restricted entitlement signs,
  verifies, and notarizes clean, then dies with SIGKILL at exec — before main,
  with no output and no log entry.

  v0.1.1 shipped exactly that way, with keychain-access-groups added for the
  destination store's shared keychain group. Every invocation of the released
  binary was killed. It was removed in 0.1.2, and two guards now stand where it
  was: scripts/release-cli-package.sh refuses a $(TeamIdentifierPrefix) in this
  file outright, and runs the packaged binary before notarizing it. Before
  adding an entitlement here, confirm it is unrestricted — or the CLI has to
  move inside a bundle first.

  The cost is real and deliberate: without a shared access group, a stream key
  the operator files in Tingra.app is invisible to the daemon. Destination
  names and URLs resolve either way (the JSON document needs no entitlement),
  a key the daemon cannot read is reported as absent, and a raw 'url'/'key'
  pair still streams. See DESTINATIONS.md, "Key sharing between the app and the
  daemon", which owns the replacement mechanism.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
